    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Georgian belongs to the small Kartvelian family with Mingrelian, Laz and Svan, and no link between this family and any other is accepted in mainstream linguistics.</li><li>It became a written language after the Kingdom of Iberia was Christianised in the mid-4th century, replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> as the literary language; its oldest literary work dates from the 5th century.</li><li>Its verbs can hold up to eight parts and mark both subject and object, and its words can open with as many as eight consonants, as in gvprtskvni, “you peel us”.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Georgian counts in twenties, as Basque does: the number 93 is otkhmotsdatsamet’i, literally “four times twenty plus thirteen”.</p></aside>
    </section>

